"serogroup":null,"clade":null,"profiled":false,"tier":"unassessed","basis":"none","tier_note":"Not assessed. This species has no curated profile here and no human-derived isolate in the ICTV record — which is not the same as evidence against human infection."},"isolates":[{"names":["rodent hepacivirus J"],"abbrev":["RHVJ"],"designation":"3382/GER/2010","exemplar":true,"accession":"KC411777","coverage":"Coding-complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV20165291"}],"geo":null,"syndromes":[],"morphology_key":"Hepaciviridae","baltimore_key":"IV","biosafety_ref":"family:Hepaciviridae"},"root/riboviria/orthornavirae/kitrinoviricota/flasuviricetes/amarillovirales/hepaciviridae/orthohepacivirus/orthohepacivirus-hominis":{"name":"Orthohepacivirus hominis","rank":"species","n_species":1,"tiers":{"established":1},"top_tier":"established","note":null,"meta":{"species":"Orthohepacivirus hominis","genus":"Orthohepacivirus","family":"Hepaciviridae","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","hosts":["Vertebrates"],"serogroup":null,"clade":null,"profiled":true,"profile":{"cycle":"blood-sexual","names":["hepatitis C virus (HCV)"],"tier":"established","headline":"Roughly 50 million chronic infections, and the only chronic viral infection of humans that a short course of oral tablets reliably cures.","syndrome":"Acute infection is asymptomatic in most and clears spontanously in about a quarter. The rest become chronic, with fibrosis progressing over decades to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Extrahepatic disease matters: mixed cryoglobulinaemia, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, porphyria cutanea tarda, and an excess of non-Hodgkin lymphoma.","transmission":"Percutaneous blood exposure above all — injecting drug use, unsafe injections and, historically, transfusion. Sexual transmission is inefficient except among men who have sex with men, particularly with HIV; vertical transmission is around 5%.","vectors":["None"],"reservoir":"Humans. The closest relatives are hepaciviruses of horses, bats and rodents; no animal reservoir maintains human infection.","geography":"Worldwide, with the highest burden in the WHO Eastern Mediterranean and European regions; Egypt's epidemic, created by mass parenteral antischistosomal therapy, was the largest ever recorded and has been largely treated away.","incubation":"2 weeks to 6 months, usually 6-9 weeks.","diagnosis":"Anti-HCV antibody, then HCV RNA to establish viraemia — antibody persists after cure, so RNA is what distinguishes current from past infection. Core antigen is an alternative where RNA is unavailable. Genotyping is now rarely needed.","management":"Pangenotypic direct-acting antivirals: sofosbuvir/velpatasvir or glecaprevir/pibrentasvir for 8-12 weeks, with sustained virological response above 95% including in compensated cirrhosis. Cure is durable and fibrosis regresses, but it confers no immunity, so reinfection is possible and surveillance continues in those with cirrhosis.","prevention":"No vaccine, and the diversity of E2 plus the absence of sterilising natural immunity make one hard. Prevention is harm reduction, needle and syringe programmes, blood and injection safety, and treatment as prevention.","evidence":["Causation established by the 1989 cloning of the agent of non-A non-B hepatitis, confirmed by chimpanzee transmission, and reinforced by the fact that curing the infection changes the clinical course. IARC Group 1."],"refs":[{"label":"Oral Direct-Acting Agent Therapy for Hepatitis C Virus Infection: A Systematic Review. Ann Intern Med 2017","pmid":"28319996","doi":"10.7326/m16-2575"},{"label":"Clinical outcomes in patients with chronic hepatitis C after direct-acting antiviral treatment: a prospective cohort study. Lancet 2019","pmid":"30765123","doi":"10.1016/s0140-6736(18)32111-1"}]},"tier":"established","basis":"profile"},"isolates":[{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 1a"],"abbrev":["HCV1a"],"designation":"H77","exemplar":true,"accession":"AF009606","coverage":"Complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 1a"],"abbrev":["HCV1a"],"designation":"PT","exemplar":false,"accession":"M62321","coverage":"Complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 1b"],"abbrev":["HCV1b"],"designation":"J","exemplar":false,"accession":"D90208","coverage":"Coding-complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 2a"],"abbrev":["HCV2a"],"designation":"HC-J6","exemplar":false,"accession":"D00944","coverage":"Coding-complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 2b"],"abbrev":["HCV2b"],"designation":"HC-J8","exemplar":false,"accession":"D10988","coverage":"Coding-complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 3a"],"abbrev":["HCV3a"],"designation":"NZL1","exemplar":false,"accession":"D17763","coverage":"Coding-complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 3k"],"abbrev":["HCV3k"],"designation":"JK049","exemplar":false,"accession":"D63821","coverage":"Coding-complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 4a"],"abbrev":["HCV4a"],"designation":"ED43","exemplar":false,"accession":"GU814265","coverage":"Coding-complete genome","genome":"ssRNA(+)","host":"vertebrates","ictv_url":"https://ictv.global/id/ICTV19910774"},{"names":["hepatitis C virus genotype 5a"],"abbrev":["HCV5a"],"designation":"EUH1480","exemplar":false,"acce